## 5. Clinical content — request (N)

5.1 Encoding rules
5.1.1 JSON SHALL be UTF-8, RFC 8259, no comments
5.1.2 Object key uniqueness, ordering, NaN/Inf prohibited
5.1.3 Numeric, string, and identifier limits
5.1.4 Forward-compatible "ignore unknown fields" rule
5.2 `SmartHealthCheckinRequest` (top level)
5.2.1 `type` (fixed discriminator)
5.2.2 `version`
5.2.3 `id` (SHALL be unique per requester per session; recommended entropy)
5.2.4 `purpose` (display, not identity)
5.2.5 `fhirVersions[]`
5.2.6 `items[]`
5.2.7 Prohibited fields (requester identity metadata)
5.2.8 (EX) minimal request, full request
5.3 `SmartHealthCheckinRequestItem`
5.3.1 `id` uniqueness, allowed character set
5.3.2 `title`
5.3.3 `summary`
5.3.4 `required` semantics (advisory only; user always controls)
5.3.5 `accept[]` (ordered preference)
5.3.6 `content` (selector — see §5.4)
5.4 Content selectors (`content.kind`)
5.4.1 `fhir.resources`
5.4.1.1 `profiles[]` — exact StructureDefinition canonicals
5.4.1.2 `profilesFrom[]` — IG / publication / profile-collection canonical URLs
5.4.1.3 `resourceTypes[]` — official FHIR resource type names
5.4.1.4 Additivity rule when both `profiles` and `profilesFrom` present
5.4.1.5 No-selector default ("any patient-specific FHIR")
5.4.1.6 (EX) US Core family, exact CARIN coverage, mixed
5.4.2 `questionnaire` selector (`content.kind = "questionnaire"`)
5.4.2.1 Flattened selector shape with sibling `canonical?` and `resource?`
5.4.2.2 `canonical` — FHIR canonical URL, with `|version` handled per §5.5
5.4.2.3 `resource` — inline FHIR `Questionnaire` resource object
5.4.2.4 Combined canonical+resource and wallet behavior when they disagree
5.4.2.5 Rejection of legacy nested `questionnaire` key shapes
5.4.2.6 (EX) inline migraine intake
5.4.3 Extension selectors — registration rules
5.5 Canonical `|version` handling
5.5.1 Structured parse into `(url, version?)` while preserving the original wire string
5.5.2 Resolver/package-cache and FHIR-search resolution semantics
5.5.3 Direct HTTP dereference limited to unversioned canonicals
5.5.4 Post-resolution verification of `resourceType`, `url`, and requested `version`
5.5.5 Decision matrix for preservation, routing, grouping, and profile matching
5.5.6 Round-trip preservation requirement on returned `meta.profile` and `QuestionnaireResponse.questionnaire`
5.6 Accepted media types (`accept[]`)
5.6.1 `application/fhir+json`
5.6.2 `application/smart-health-card`
5.6.3 Extension media types — registration rules
5.6.4 `accept` ordering semantics

## 6. Clinical content — response (N)

6.1 `SmartHealthCheckinResponse` (top level)
6.1.1 `type`
6.1.2 `version`
6.1.3 `requestId` SHALL equal request `id`
6.1.4 `artifacts[]`
6.1.5 `requestStatus[]`
6.2 Artifact common shape
6.2.1 `id` uniqueness within response
6.2.2 `mediaType`
6.2.3 `fulfills[]` (SHALL be non-empty, ids SHALL exist in request)
6.2.4 Body field constraints defined by each pinned `mediaType` variant
6.3 Concrete artifact shapes
6.3.1 SMART Health Card artifact (`application/smart-health-card`)
6.3.1.1 `value.verifiableCredential[]` JWS list
6.3.1.2 No outer `fhirVersion` rule
6.3.2 Raw FHIR JSON artifact (`application/fhir+json`)
6.3.2.1 `fhirVersion` SHALL be present
6.3.2.2 Bundle / single-resource shape rules
6.3.2.3 Mixed-version-Bundle prohibition
6.3.3 Registered extension artifacts: branded variants with pinned `mediaType` and typed shape (no `GenericArtifact`)
6.3.4 (EX) SMART Health Card and raw FHIR JSON artifacts
6.4 Status reporting
6.4.1 `requestStatus[]` SHALL cover every request item exactly once
6.4.2 Status code registry — `fulfilled` / `partial` / `unavailable` / `declined` / `unsupported` / `error`
6.4.3 Optional `message`
6.5 Many-to-many fulfillment
6.5.1 One artifact may fulfill many items
6.5.2 One item may be fulfilled by many artifacts
6.5.3 (EX) shared QuestionnaireResponse
6.6 Cross-validation rules verifier SHALL apply
6.6.1 `requestId` match
6.6.2 `fulfills[]` references resolve
6.6.3 Artifact `mediaType` ∈ each fulfilled item's `accept[]`
6.6.4 `requestStatus` covers items uniquely
6.6.5 FHIR version consistency for `application/fhir+json`
6.6.6 Bundle / `meta.profile` checks (informative guidance)

## 7. Trust framework (N)

7.1 Origin trust
7.1.1 Browser-asserted web origin (when DC API exposes it)
7.1.2 Wallet-side privileged-caller / browser-trust policy (package + signing-cert fingerprint where applicable; deployment-defined)
7.1.3 Behavior when origin cannot be authenticated
7.2 Reader / Verifier trust
7.2.1 Optional `readerAuth` COSE_Sign1 over `ReaderAuthentication`
7.2.2 Reader certificate chain (`x5chain`) + trust anchor policy
7.2.3 Wallet handling of unsigned vs signed reader requests
7.3 Issuer / device-attestation trust (mdoc binding)
7.3.1 MSO (issuer-signed) trust anchors — IACA-style or registry-based
7.3.2 Device key proof of possession
7.3.3 "Self-attested wallet" model (no external issuer trust required)
7.4 Source trust on clinical content
7.4.1 SMART Health Card chain of custody (signed credential payload)
7.4.2 Raw FHIR JSON treated as patient-mediated (binding by transport, not provenance)
7.5 Identifier scoping & uniqueness (request id, response id, artifact id)
7.6 Out-of-band trust establishment (deployment policy)

## 8. Same-device presentation flow over `org-iso-mdoc` (N)

8.1 Identifiers (registered in §13)
8.1.1 Protocol id `org-iso-mdoc`
8.1.2 mdoc `docType` `org.smarthealthit.checkin.1`
8.1.3 Namespace `org.smarthealthit.checkin`
8.1.4 Stable element `smart_health_checkin_response`
8.1.5 Request carrier claim `org.smarthealthit.checkin.request` (in `requestInfo`)
8.1.6 HPKE suite & COSE alg
8.2 Verifier-side request construction
8.2.1 SMART JSON serialization for `requestInfo`
8.2.2 `ItemsRequest` shape (docType, nameSpaces, requestInfo)
8.2.3 `intentToRetain` default = `true` (override conditions)
8.2.4 `DeviceRequest` v1.0 shape; v1.1 `readerAuthAll` not used
8.2.5 Tag-24 wrapping invariant
8.2.6 (Optional) per-DocRequest `readerAuth`
8.2.7 HPKE recipient keypair
8.2.8 `encryptionInfo` CBOR construction (`["dcapi", {nonce, recipientPublicKey}]`)
8.2.9 `navigator.credentials.get(...)` argument (base64url-no-pad fields)
8.2.10 (EX) full request bytes ladder
8.3 SessionTranscript construction (both sides SHALL compute identically)
8.3.1 `dcapiInfo = CBOR([encryptionInfoBase64Url, origin])`
8.3.2 `handover = ["dcapi", SHA-256(dcapiInfo)]`
8.3.3 `SessionTranscript = CBOR([null, null, handover])`
8.3.4 Origin acquisition rules and platform-neutral requirements
8.4 Wallet-side request handling
8.4.1 Recover and validate `ItemsRequest`
8.4.2 Decode SMART request JSON from `requestInfo`
8.4.3 Recompute SessionTranscript
8.4.4 Verify optional `readerAuth`
8.4.5 Run consent UI (per-item user choice)
8.5 Wallet-side response construction
8.5.1 Build SMART response JSON
8.5.2 `IssuerSignedItem` (digestID, random, elementIdentifier, elementValue)
8.5.3 Tag-24 wrap; SHA-256 digest
8.5.4 MSO contents + tag-24 payload + COSE_Sign1
8.5.5 `DeviceAuthentication` payload (with SessionTranscript)
8.5.6 Device COSE_Sign1
8.5.7 `DeviceResponse` envelope
8.6 HPKE encryption
8.6.1 Suite (`DHKEM(P-256, HKDF-SHA256)`, `HKDF-SHA256`, `AES-128-GCM`)
8.6.2 `info` = SessionTranscript bytes
8.6.3 `aad` = empty
8.6.4 `enc` (encapsulated key) format
8.6.5 `dcapiResponse` CBOR envelope (`["dcapi", {enc, cipherText}]`)
8.6.6 Outer DC API wrapper (`{"protocol":"org-iso-mdoc","data":{"response":...}}`)
8.7 Verifier-side processing
8.7.1 HPKE-open
8.7.2 Walk `DeviceResponse`
8.7.3 Verify `issuerAuth` (cert chain → trust anchor)
8.7.4 Re-hash `IssuerSignedItem` and compare against MSO digest
8.7.5 Verify `deviceSignature` against MSO `deviceKey`
8.7.6 Decode SMART response JSON; apply §6.6 cross-checks
8.8 Required validation checklist (verifier)
8.9 (EX) Annotated end-to-end byte capture (links to fixtures in Appendix D)


